"The Shrewd Dude" <Shrewd.Dude@gmail.com> wrote:
> Janis Papanagnou wrote:
>> Colin wrote:

>>> I'm a novice player (almost always die before
>>> level 5 novice) and I have a lot of problems
>>> managing my inventory at the beginning of the
>>> game.

Very likely your problem is choosing what to drop.

>>> I tend to get burdened because I'm carrying
>>> around a lot of stuff that I haven't had the
>>> chance to identify yet.

>>> Two questions:

>>> 1) what items should I leave behind initially,

Yep.

Don't think of things that way.

Try zero-based inventory budgeting, instead:

>>> and

what you _need_ to carry with you from turn to turn,
[to limit unavoidable damage, conduct melee, and
avoid starving while cornered away from food and
running from monsters], rather than come back to and
use at a convenient time, is pretty much armor, a
weapon, and nourishment.

_Anything_ else is suspect to have in your inventory
if you are burdened or near to burdened, including
extras of any of those three types, or overly heavy
instances of those three types.

The latter means to ditch plate mail in favor of
worse AC, you'll live longer if you can outrun the
monsters, to ditch dwarven mattocks in favor of
daggers, again you can run away faster, to ditch
food rations past the second, and to eat the
heaviest types of food first.

With this approach, most objects never get picked up
until just about to be used up, or just long enough
to carry them to some storage location.

Notice that pet management is a big part of staying
unburdened. A buff, healthy pet, in the early game,
can replace both armor class and weaponry, by
fighting the monsters instead of you doing so,
reducing your PCs need for heavy armor or weapons.

At higher PC levels and deeper dungeon levels than
you are reaching, though, your original pets stop
being very useful, because the monsters are now
beyond their capacity, but they'll carry you pretty
well up to about your PC level 7, if you don't go
very deep in the dungeon.

>>> 2) what's a safe way to leave things behind so
>>> that other creatures won't take them?

Monsters don't open or loot containers, even bags,
though some big monsters pick up containers for a
while and drop them later; also, at higher
difficulties than you're surviving to yet, some
monsters _eat_ containers. worry about those when
you get that far.

>> Leave behind all the heavy items, mainly
>> redundant armor and weapons, but also spellbooks
>> (if you are playing a spellcaster, you may want
>> to read them prior)

Nah, just set them aside until you identify an ID
scroll, then bless that and try IDing spellbooks;
alternately, once the PC is level 3 or more, it is
(fairly) safe when HP are maxed to read spellbooks;
best is to lock oneself in a closet, drop money and
anything else burnable than the one scrollbook, then
read the scrollbook, after the pet confirms that it
is at least uncursed. Best is still to stall off
until you can bless every spellbook before reading
it, now infallibly IIUC.

> Spellbook-reading should be done with caution, as
> they can teleport you, blind you, paralyze you,
> and do other nasty things to you, especially if
> they're cursed. Any of those things can kill you
> easily in the early game.
 
>> and plain gray stones.

> You should be careful that you're not throwing
> away a luckstone.

Or a touchstone. Or trying to throw away a
loadstone. A good strategy is to name the class of
gray stone objects you first encounter as, say,
"gray #1", so that you can tell if the next gray
stone you find is some other type even before
risking picking it up. Later, if you price ID a gray
stone and it isn't dirt cheap, you have one that
isn't a flint stone, and if you're lucky, it might
be a type you now know you've seen before, and can
go retrieve the other one too.

>> You'll often find chests and boxes that are a
>> safe place where monsters won't look into. You
>> may kick open chests that are locked (though that
>> can destroy glass items that have been in the
>> container).

Better is to get a pet-approved crude dagger
death-dropped from a goblin, a very common item to
acquire in the first level or two, and use it to
#force the lock, which doesn't destroy items in the
chest, though the dagger may break with some small
chance.

> You can also use a lockpick, or try #forcing the
> lock.

>> Another option is pushing a boulder onto a stack
>> of items.
 
> Be careful with this, as giants CAN move boulders.

They don't normally appear for level 5 PCs, though.
This OP wants to know how to survive long enough to
worry about those nastier monsters.
 
>> Locking items in a one square niche is also
>> possible.

> Some creatures can open doors, and IIRC, doors
> can't be locked again once you've opened them.

Doors can be locked, if intact, with a key. Even
if knocked down, there's some possibility of
(repairing and) locking them, will a spell of wizard
lock, but that's pretty unlikely to be available to
a low level PC.

> If you don't have the force bolt spell, a pickaxe,
> or some other way of breaking rock, you especially
> don't want to combine two methods of stuffing your
> stuff in a closet and blocking it with a boulder.

Maybe. If what you are stashing for now isn't
something you need immediately, it isn't something
you need at some set point in the future, either.
There's plenty of time to come back for it once a
pickaxe or mattock is in hand.

I find that my most secure "stash" is a box, chest,
or bag (I can rarely even _lift_ ice chests), set
beside the top of a down stairway I am about to use,
on top of an Elbereth to prevent monsters from
taking the chest, and topped with a cursed object
to prevent my pets from taking the chest.

Second choice is to drop the objects in a shop, in a
stack, but refuse the shopkeeper's offer to buy the
objects, then leave the shop door locked. Shop walls
are self repairing, which helps the protection a bit
compared to say closets, which once penetrated are
forever accessible.

In both situations, getting off the level
fast/immediately is the rest of the strategy, since 
the game logic stops all activity on a level while
the PC is not there, so nothing can get stolen
while you are gone, only while you are wandering
around the level.

